#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168040 :tutorial/content "When an integrand contains a product of trigonometric functions, we can often rewrite that product as a sum using the sum and difference formulas. Then, the integral becomes a sum of simpler integrals.\n\nFirst, let's recall the sum and difference formulas for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta ) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\beta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\beta  \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta ) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\beta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\beta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these equations, we get \n\n$$\n\\cos(\\alpha-\\beta) + \\cos(\\alpha+\\beta) = 2 \\cos \\alpha \\cos \\beta.\n$$\n\nNow, solving for $\\cos \\alpha \\cos \\beta,$ we obtain the following **product-to-sum identity**:\n\n$$\n\\cos \\alpha \\cos \\beta = \\dfrac{1}{2}\\left(\\cos(\\alpha-\\beta)+\\cos(\\alpha+\\beta)\\right)\n$$\n\nFor example, consider the integral\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\cos(5x)\\cos(3x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nUsing the identity above,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x) & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x-3x)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x+3x)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(8x)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(8x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+\\frac{1}{16}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(8x)+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an arbitrary constant." :tutorial/id #uuid "c26de959-d4f8-59d7-987a-b054cf26fe18" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14665 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168041 :tutorial/content "Similar to the above, when an integrand contains a product of sines, we can rewrite that product as a sum of cosines using the sum and difference formulas for cosine. Then, the integral becomes a sum of simpler integrals.\n\nFirst, let's recall the sum and difference formulas for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta ) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\beta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\beta  \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta ) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\beta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\beta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubtracting the second equation from the first, we get\n\n$$\n\\cos(\\alpha-\\beta) - \\cos(\\alpha+\\beta) = 2 \\sin \\alpha \\sin \\beta.\n$$\n\nNow, solving for $\\sin \\alpha \\sin \\beta,$ we obtain the following *product-to-sum identity*:\n\n$$\n\\sin \\alpha \\sin \\beta = \\dfrac{1}{2}\\left(\\cos(\\alpha-\\beta)-\\cos(\\alpha+\\beta)\\right)\n$$\n\nFor example, consider the integral\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\sin(7x)\\sin(4x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nUsing the identity above,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x) & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7x-4x)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7x+4x)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(11x)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡(7x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(11x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)-\\frac{1}{22}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(11x)+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an arbitrary constant." :tutorial/id #uuid "0b307dbf-5537-546f-8a0c-3ad325959aca" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14666 :tutorial/title "Integrating a Product of Sines"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168042 :tutorial/content "Next, when an integrand contains a product of sine and cosine, we can rewrite that product as a sum of sines using the sum and difference formulas for sine. Then, the integral becomes a sum of simpler integrals.\n\nFirst, let's recall the sum and difference formulas for sine:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta ) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\beta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\beta  \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta ) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\beta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\beta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these equations, we get\n\n$$\n\\sin(\\alpha+\\beta) + \\sin(\\alpha-\\beta) = 2 \\sin \\alpha \\cos \\beta.\n$$\n\nNow, solving for $\\sin \\alpha \\cos \\beta,$ we obtain the following *product-to-sum identity*:\n\n$$\n\\sin \\alpha \\cos \\beta = \\dfrac{1}{2}\\left(\\sin(\\alpha+\\beta)+\\sin(\\alpha-\\beta)\\right)\n$$\n\nFor example, consider the integral\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\sin(8x)\\cos(2x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nUsing the identity above,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(8x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x) & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(8x+2x)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(8x-2x)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(10x)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6x)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡(8x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(10x)+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{20}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(10x)-\\frac{1}{12}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x)+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an arbitrary constant." :tutorial/id #uuid "1e3af3c5-49cd-524e-8614-b45a35212820" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14667 :tutorial/title "Integrating a Product of Sine and Cosine"}]}
